@[system]

※※ Clocks: what time it is by each the system keeps, and how finely each tells it.
※
※ Imported by `std` and handed on, so a program knows it as `std.time`.  The two
※ functions are POSIX's `clock_gettime` and `clock_getres`, and `Timespec` is
※ C's `struct timespec`: seconds, and nanoseconds within the second.  A compiled
※ program reads the clocks through the functions the kernel maps into every
※ process, the vDSO, found through the auxiliary vector it was started with, and
※ asks the kernel itself where those are not there -- a shared object, which has
※ no start of its own to read the vector at, always asks.  The interpreter reads
※ them through the C library.

※※ A time, as C's `struct timespec` holds one.
※※
※※ \details `tv_sec` is whole seconds and `tv_nsec` the nanoseconds past them,
※※ from nought to 999999999.  Which seconds they are counted from is the
※※ clock's: the epoch of 1970 for `Clock.realtime`, an unspecified moment for
※※ the others.
@[export, abi]
type Timespec = tv_sec : i64 ; tv_nsec : i64

※※ The clocks Linux keeps, with the numbers its kernel gives them, which are the
※※ same on every architecture.
※※
※※ \details `realtime` is the time of day, which may be set and may jump;
※※ `monotonic` never goes back and does not count a suspended system;
※※ `boottime` is `monotonic` that does; `monotonic_raw` is `monotonic` without
※※ the corrections of a time protocol; `tai` is `realtime` without leap seconds;
※※ the two `_coarse` ones are cheaper and coarser; and `process_cputime` and
※※ `thread_cputime` count the processor time of the process and of the thread.
@[export]
enum Clock : i32:
    realtime = 0 ;
    monotonic = 1 ;
    process_cputime = 2 ;
    thread_cputime = 3 ;
    monotonic_raw = 4 ;
    realtime_coarse = 5 ;
    monotonic_coarse = 6 ;
    boottime = 7 ;
    tai = 11

※ What the runtime offers: the time or the resolution written where it is
※ handed, and nought or the kernel's error negated.
@[external("pl4g_clock_gettime"), io]
fn gettime(clock: Clock, into: &mut Timespec) → i64

@[external("pl4g_clock_getres"), io]
fn getres(clock: Clock, into: &mut Timespec) → i64

※ The error number of what the runtime answered, nought for none.
fn error_of(got: i64) → i32:
    if got ≥ 0i64: 0i32 else: ⎕narrow(0i64 - got, ⌜i32⌝) ?? 22i32

※※ What time `clock` says it is, written into `into`: POSIX's `clock_gettime`.
※※
※※ \details A `Timespec` is laid out as C's is, so it crosses as a reference --
※※ which is what C's own `clock_gettime` takes too.
※※ \param clock which clock to read
※※ \param into where the time is written
※※ \return nought, or the system's error number where the clock cannot be read
※※ -- 22 (`EINVAL`) for one this system does not keep -- `into` then unchanged
@[export, io]
fn clock_gettime(clock: Clock, into: &mut Timespec) → i32:
    error_of(gettime(clock, into))

※※ How finely `clock` tells the time, written into `into`: POSIX's
※※ `clock_getres`.
※※
※※ \param clock which clock to ask
※※ \param into where the smallest step the clock takes is written
※※ \return nought, or the system's error number
@[export, io]
fn clock_getres(clock: Clock, into: &mut Timespec) → i32:
    error_of(getres(clock, into))
